// Lesson 6.1 — Gravity & Jump: The First Flick
// Unit 6 opens in the cave. Nothing in the starter jumps: PlayerController.cs is
// an empty stub, and the Rigidbody2D the student built in Unit 2 was tuned for a
// top-down world with Gravity Scale 0. So this lesson is two new mechanisms, and
// at level 1 each gets its own walkthrough: turning gravity on for ONE scene, and
// the flick that leaves the ground.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (verified by reading the project, its scenes and its ProjectSettings):
//   - Unity 6000.6.0f1. Legacy Input class (ProjectSettings/InputManager.asset has
//     the classic axes), so Input.GetKeyDown is correct. Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity,
//     not the old `velocity`. Object.FindFirstObjectByType<T>() over FindObjectOfType.
//   - EditorBuildSettings.asset orders the scenes Start(0), Overworld(1),
//     Platformer(2) — "the cave is index 2" is real, and SceneSwitch.cs holds a
//     serialized int build index, not a name.
//   - Physics2DSettings.asset ships m_Gravity {x: 0, y: -9.81}. So "the project-wide
//     gravity is -9.81 on Y" is checkable, and Gravity Scale really is a multiplier
//     on it.
//   - Player.prefab, READ AS A PREFAB (the scenes carry stale overrides, so the
//     prefab is the only honest source for what exists): the root carries a
//     Transform and PlayerController; the SpriteRenderer and Animator live on a
//     child called `Sprite`. There is NO Rigidbody2D and NO Collider2D on it —
//     those are the student's work from 2.1, and 2.1 teaches Body Type Dynamic with
//     Gravity Scale 0. So the lesson's premise (the body has Gravity Scale 0) holds.
//   - THE FINDING: the Platformer scene holds the Player as a PREFAB INSTANCE whose
//     modification list carries `propertyPath: isPlatformer / value: 1` targeting
//     the PlayerController MonoBehaviour. The field does not exist in the stripped
//     PlayerController.cs, so today it does nothing — a leftover from the project's
//     own build, and the project's own answer to "which world am I in?" (option (c):
//     a serialized bool ticked per scene instance). Unity keys those overrides by
//     field NAME, so declaring a serialized field with exactly that name picks the
//     tick up; naming it anything else loses it. The lesson says so and tells the
//     student to check the box rather than trusting it.
//   - Likewise the Overworld's Player instance overrides m_GravityScale = 0, and the
//     Rigidbody2D it targets no longer exists in the stripped prefab. Same story:
//     evidence that per-scene gravity is the project's intent, and proof the
//     top-down Player is gravity-off.
//   - Main Camera in the Platformer carries CameraController (Assets/Scripts/Package
//     Scripts/CameraController.cs) with offset (0, 2.5) and lockY = 1. So the Bug
//     Hunt's camera experiment is real: the camera does NOT follow a jump upward,
//     and `lockY` is the field holding it still. Do not call the cave's ground body
//     "Gravity Scale 0" — the Collision tilemap is a Static body whose Inspector
//     reads Gravity Scale 1, greyed out (6.2's frontmatter says the same).
//   - SceneSwitch.cs really does switch scenes from OnCollisionEnter2D, so the
//     Kinematic row of the technique table is right that a Kinematic Player would
//     make the cave door go silent.
//
// SHAPE (rewritten from the old API-table + five-questions Build Lab):
//   Scaffolding is LEVEL 1 - FULL WALKTHROUGH, and asks.ts agrees ("how do I jump?",
//   level 1). Gravity, vertical velocity and the flick have no precedent anywhere in
//   this course, so this is the full treatment: zero gaps in EITHER walkthrough, and
//   all five decompose parts given with `does` + `doc`.
//   - renderDecompose('6.1', ..., 1) opens the body, like 5.2. Five parts: the body
//     that falls, the flag that says which world this is, the once-only write in
//     Start(), the key press, and the flick gravity then spends. Every part has a
//     reading; none is blank.
//   - TWO renderWalkthrough calls, both level 1 (a lesson's walkthroughs must
//     declare the same rung):
//       WALK_GRAVITY  PlayerController.cs - the scene flag, the gated gravity scale
//                     in Start(), the Inspector tick on the SCENE instance, and the
//                     second Play run that proves the overworld survived.
//       WALK_JUMP     PlayerController.cs - the strength field, GetKeyDown, the
//                     AddForce impulse, and the Console line that shows the arc.
//                     It assumes the gravity half and says so.
//   - WALK_GRAVITY's finalFile is the gravity half with the rest elided (5.2's
//     idiom); WALK_JUMP's is the whole file. Both are written in a different-but-
//     equivalent form from their own steps - a ternary that decides the number for
//     both worlds instead of an `if` that writes it for one, and the flick in a
//     named method instead of inline - so comparing files is reading for meaning,
//     not diffing text. Nothing a later lesson depends on is renamed: 6.2 reads
//     "the key went down, add an impulse", and 6.3 asks about "the scene-mode flag
//     from 6.1", so both survive here by name.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS - ids are POSITIONAL, so nothing here is reordered, renumbered
