// PlayerController.cs - gravity for the cave, and one flick upward.
// The gravity half came first; the jump assumes it. Up, apex, down: the
// only line that makes the arc is the force, and gravity does the rest.
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;      // your number from 2.2
    [SerializeField] private float jumpForce = 7f;      // how hard the flick is
    [SerializeField] private bool isPlatformer = false; // which world did I wake up in?

    private Rigidbody2D body;

    void Start()
    {
        // Looked up once, never inside Update() - the rule from 2.2.
        body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

        // Decided once, for both worlds.
        body.gravityScale = isPlatformer ? 1f : 0f;
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // ... input, movement and animation, from 1.4 to 5.3 ...

        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space))
        {
            Jump();
        }
    }

    // The whole jump. One impulse, and gravity spends it over the next half
    // second - which is why there is nothing here about coming back down.
    private void Jump()
    {
        body.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. Two differences are worth naming, and neither is a mistake. First the shape: the walkthrough wrote the push inline inside the key check, and this file gives it a method called `Jump()` - the same line, with a name you can point at when 6.2 and 6.3 start asking what a jump is allowed to do. Second the gravity line: this file decides the number for both worlds with a ternary where the walkthrough only wrote it for one. What has to match is the shape of the jump: a single-frame key check, one `AddForce` with `ForceMode2D.Impulse`, and a world that is already pulling down before you press anything. Your key, your number, and the names of your own methods are yours.',
};

const body = `<h1>6.1 — Gravity &amp; Jump: The First Flick</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The cave is the first place in this game where <em>down</em> means something. Your
    character has spent five units as a top-down walker with gravity switched off — and it
    shows. Walk off the edge of the cave floor and you do not fall: you keep walking, level,
    for as long as you hold the key. Today you turn gravity back on for one scene, and then
    you use the same body to throw the character into the air.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I jump?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('6.1', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: The Cave Has No Down</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Your goal: fall, then jump.</strong> Open
        <code>Assets/_Scenes/Platformer.unity</code> — the cave — and press Play. Walk left
        and right: that still works, because the movement you built in
        <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a> and rewired in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> does not care
        what the world looks like. Now walk off the edge of the floor and watch.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing happens. You do not fall, and you do not jump. And neither of those is a bug
        in your code — they are both consequences of a decision <em>you</em> made two units
        ago. The Rigidbody2D you gave the Player in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> has
        <strong>Gravity Scale 0</strong>, because a top-down character who obeys gravity
        falls through the world forever. You were right then. The cave is the first place in
        the game where you are wrong.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Discover it before you write anything:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Select the Player <em>while the game is running</em>. Find Gravity Scale on the
            Rigidbody2D, set it to <code>1</code>, and watch what the character does. Then
            set it to <code>0.2</code>. Describe the difference out loud.</li>
        <li>Stop Play mode. Look at Gravity Scale again. What happened to the number you just
            typed? Inspector edits during Play are thrown away on Stop — which is exactly why
            the next step has to be code, not a checkbox.</li>
        <li>Now select the Player in the <code>Overworld</code> scene — the top-down hub — and
            set Gravity Scale to 1 there, then press Play. This is the trap in the other
            direction. Write down what it costs.</li>
        <li>Open the cave scene, select the Player standing in it, and look at
            <code>PlayerController</code> in the Inspector. <strong>The project has left you
            the answer.</strong> Read on — this one needs a paragraph of its own.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Somebody already decided how this script learns which scene it is in.</strong>
        The cave scene's copy of the Player has been carrying an override called
        <code>isPlatformer</code> since before this course existed. It is a leftover from the
        version of <code>PlayerController.cs</code> that shipped with the project, and it is
        invisible today because the stub you were given has no field by that name. But it is
        evidence, and it is worth knowing how to read: the project's own answer to
        "which world am I in?" was <em>a serialized bool, ticked on the scene copy</em>.
        Unity keys those overrides by field <strong>name</strong>, so declaring a field with
        exactly that name normally brings the tick back with it. You will do that below —
        and you will check the box rather than trust it.
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Two physics facts, and they are independent.</strong> A body only falls if it
        is <strong>Dynamic</strong> <em>and</em> its Gravity Scale is above zero — either one
        alone does nothing, and each of them is a setting you already own. And a jump is not
        "move up": it is a <em>change in velocity</em> that physics then spends over the next
        half second. Gravity and jumping are the same mechanic seen from two sides.
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Field on the Rigidbody2D</th><th>What it actually does</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Body Type</code></td>
            <td>Dynamic bodies fall and are pushed. Kinematic bodies only move where code puts them and <strong>ignore gravity, forces and mass entirely</strong>. Static never moves. If your character stands still in mid-air with Gravity Scale 5, look here first — a Static or Kinematic body is the only thing that can ignore a gravity scale that is plainly set.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Gravity Scale</code></td>
            <td>A <strong>multiplier</strong> on the project-wide gravity (Project Settings, Physics 2D, Y at <code>-9.81</code>). 0 is off; 1 is Earth; about 0.17 is the moon; 3 is a dropped anvil. It only means anything on a Dynamic body.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Mass</code></td>
            <td>How much the <em>same</em> force moves you. Gravity pulls harder on a heavy body, so mass partly cancels itself out and the fall barely changes — but a force you apply by hand divides by mass. Raise mass and your jump gets lower while your fall stays roughly the same.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Linear Damping</code></td>
            <td>Drag. Bleeds off speed over time, in the air as well as on the ground. Useful for a floaty "air resistance" feel; a value of 0 keeps your arc a clean parabola.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Constraints</code></td>
            <td>Freeze Rotation Z is the one you set in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> so your character stops tumbling. It still matters in the air: an unfrozen body spins when it clips a corner mid-jump, and the sprite spins with it.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Gravity Is a Number You Own</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Real gravity is a constant of the universe. Unity ships it as an <em>Inspector field</em>. Why would a game engine make a law of physics editable per object?</li>
            <li>Your top-down scene needs Gravity Scale 0 and your cave needs it above 0. Same Player prefab, same script, two different worlds. Where should that difference live — in the script, in the scene, or in both?</li>
            <li>A Kinematic body ignores gravity, forces and mass entirely. If you set Body Type to Kinematic in the cave, what would your jump code have to do instead to look like a jump?</li>
            <li>Mass barely changes how fast you <em>fall</em> but strongly changes how high you <em>jump</em>. Explain why one number treats the two cases differently.</li>
            <li>You can hold a body in the air forever by leaving Gravity Scale at 0 in the cave. Name a game genre where that is a feature rather than a bug.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        None of these is from a video game, and none of them is more than a line. Read each
        one for what it does in isolation, then decide how it maps onto a jump — the last
        three are the ones worth arguing with, because they are about <em>when</em> a number
        matters rather than what it is.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — The difference between a hand and a flick (bowling alley):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// A steady push applied every physics step is a hand on the ball.
// An impulse is one flick. Same total push. Completely different sport.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A forklift lifting a pallet:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>liftBody.AddForce(Vector2.up * forkStrength, ForceMode2D.Impulse); // one shove</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — A submarine blowing its ballast tanks:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>hull.linearVelocity = new Vector2(hull.linearVelocity.x, riseRate); // keep drift, set rise</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A weather balloon (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// A balloon is not "anti-gravity". It is a body with a tiny gravity scale
// and a huge drag, so it rises slowly and stops rising.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A trampoline inspection rig (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The height of a bounce is a velocity the moment the feet leave the mat.
// Everything after that is gravity spending it.</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
<li>In the forklift example, what would change if you removed the <code>ForceMode2D.Impulse</code> argument? Predict the behaviour in one sentence, then test both in your cave.</li>
<li>The submarine keeps its horizontal drift and overwrites only the vertical part. What does that tell you about which component of a velocity a jump should touch — and which component it must leave alone?</li>
<li>The balloon rises with a tiny gravity scale, and your top-down world used gravity scale 0. What is the difference between <em>nothing falls</em> and <em>something rises</em>?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: How Does a Jump Get Into the Air?</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        There is no <code>Jump()</code> in Unity, and nobody is going to give you one. There
        are four defensible ways to get a Dynamic body off the ground. Read all four before
        you pick — the walkthrough below builds the first row, and you are going to live with
        your choice for the rest of the year.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it requires</th><th>What it feels like</th><th>The cost</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)</code></td>
            <td>One instant change in momentum. You never touch velocity yourself; physics spends it over the following frames.</td>
            <td>A Dynamic body and Gravity Scale above 0.</td>
            <td>The classic flick. Arc is a clean parabola.</td>
            <td>Mass divides into it, so retuning mass silently retunes every jump in the game.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>linearVelocity = new Vector2(x, jumpSpeed)</code></td>
            <td>You overwrite the vertical half of the velocity directly. Physics still owns everything after that.</td>
            <td>A Dynamic body and Gravity Scale above 0.</td>
            <td>Identical height every single time, to the pixel.</td>
            <td>It erases whatever vertical motion was already there — a moving platform's lift, a knockback, a bounce.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AddForce(Vector2.up * thrust, ForceMode2D.Force)</code> every frame while held</td>
            <td>A continuous push, applied each physics step the key is down. This is what you get by accident if you forget the force mode.</td>
            <td>A Dynamic body; some rule that stops it (fuel, ceiling, timer).</td>
            <td>A rocket, not a jump. Height depends on how long you hold.</td>
            <td>It is flight. Without a limit your player leaves the level and never comes back.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>MovePosition()</code> in steps</td>
            <td>Code hands the body a target position and it moves there in steps. It never has a velocity you set — and a Kinematic body arrives at the target whatever it passes through on the way.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Kinematic</strong> body, moved in <code>FixedUpdate</code>.</td>
            <td>Perfectly controlled, weightless, and no arc.</td>
            <td>A Kinematic body ignores gravity, so it never falls — and it only collides with Dynamic bodies, so no wall, floor or platform is solid to it and no <code>OnCollision*</code> message fires against those static colliders. The cave door that switches scenes runs on <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code> and would go silent. You would be writing gravity, walls and collision messages yourself — the things you came here to stop doing.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong><code>ForceMode2D.Force</code> versus <code>ForceMode2D.Impulse</code> in one
        sentence.</strong> Force is a push that keeps applying while you keep calling it;
        Impulse is a single hit. Forget the argument and you get Force — which is a dribble,
        not a jump. That is the single most common "my jump does nothing" bug in this unit.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choose, Then Justify</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the four did you choose, and what does it require you to set on the Rigidbody2D that the other three do not?</li>
<li>Row two gives the same height every time; row one gives a height that depends on mass. Which is easier to explain to a teammate in six months, and which is easier to retune?</li>
<li>Row three is the only one where holding the key keeps mattering. Is that a jump or a jetpack — and what would have to be true about your level design for a jetpack to be fair?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK_GRAVITY)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK_JUMP)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Turn Gravity On, Then Jump</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 0 — inventory.</strong> Select the Player in the cave scene and list what
        is on it: Transform, <code>PlayerController</code>, whatever you added in Unit 2 — and
        the child <code>Sprite</code> carrying the SpriteRenderer and Animator. Confirm a
        <strong>Rigidbody2D</strong> and a <strong>Collider2D</strong> are both there. If you
        added them to a scene instance instead of the prefab in Unit 2, the prefab may still
        be bare — fix that first, or every scene you build from here inherits the problem.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: how does your script know it is in the cave?</strong>
        Your movement code currently assumes a top-down world. The cave needs the opposite
        assumptions, and the script has to be told which world it woke up in. The walkthrough
        built the third option below, because that is the one the project itself left evidence
        of. Options, all of them used in real projects:
        <ul>
            <li><strong>(a) Ask the scene its name</strong> — <code>SceneManager.GetActiveScene().name</code> compared against <code>"Platformer"</code> in <code>Start()</code>. Readable, and it breaks silently if someone renames the scene.</li>
            <li><strong>(b) Ask the scene its build index</strong> — the cave is index 2. Numbers do not break when a scene is renamed, but they lie when the build order changes. <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> already uses this for scene switching, so read it before you borrow the idea.</li>
            <li><strong>(c) A serialized bool on the prefab, ticked per scene instance</strong> — you can see it in the Inspector without pressing Play, and this is what the cave scene's leftover <code>isPlatformer</code> override says the project chose. The cost is that every new scene needs someone to remember to tick it.</li>
            <li><strong>(d) A separate component that only exists on the cave's Player</strong> — the component's presence <em>is</em> the flag. Costs one more file and a <code>GetComponent</code>.</li>
            <li>Whatever you pick: if you already built a scene-mode flag earlier in the year, reuse it. Two flags that both mean "which world am I in" is how a project starts rotting.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: gravity.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Where does the cave's gravity get turned on? Name the exact point in the lifecycle — a field default, <code>Start()</code>, or every frame — and say what breaks at each of the other two.</li>
            <li>If you write <code>gravityScale = 1</code> inside <code>Update()</code> unconditionally, what happens to the top-down Overworld? Go and check rather than guessing.</li>
            <li>You could skip the code entirely: select the cave copy, type <code>1</code> into Gravity Scale, and select the Overworld copy and type <code>0</code>. It works. Do it once, then write one sentence on what a reader of your script now cannot see.</li>
            <li>What value do you start with for the cave — 1, 3, 0.5? Write down the number and one word for the feel, before you tune it.</li>
            <li>Your movement line from 2.2 reads <code>Vertical</code> as well as <code>Horizontal</code>, so pressing W in the cave still lifts the character straight up. Your flag is already the gate for that. You do not have to fix it today — but write down where the fix would go.</li>
            <li>Is your top-down scene still correct after this change? The test is not "does the cave work" — it is "does the <em>other</em> scene still work".</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the flick.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which key, and which of <code>GetKey</code> or <code>GetKeyDown</code>? What does holding the key for two seconds do under each one?</li>
            <li>Where does the <code>Rigidbody2D</code> reference come from, and when do you cache it? Does it live on the same GameObject as your script, or on the child <code>Sprite</code>? Look before you type.</li>
            <li>Does the jump belong in <code>Update()</code> or <code>FixedUpdate()</code>? The key check and the force have different answers — say which is which and why.</li>
            <li>What is the magnitude — a serialized field so a designer can tune it, or a magic number? And does it name a <em>force</em> or a <em>speed</em>? The name you give it decides what the next person thinks it does.</li>
            <li>How do you know it worked? Write the Console line that proves the jump fired, and the second one that proves gravity is pulling you back down.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>The tuning triangle.</strong> Change one number at a time and write one
        sentence of feel for each, in a file you keep:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Knob</th><th>Try</th><th>The sentence you write</th></tr>
        <tr><td>jump force</td><td>half, then double</td><td>"how high" — arcade versus ant</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gravity Scale</td><td>0.2 &rarr; 1 &rarr; 4</td><td>"how heavy" — moon versus dropped stone</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mass</td><td>1 &rarr; 5</td><td>how much the same push moves you — and whether your fall changed at all</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Gravity off plus an impulse is not a jump — it is an escape.</strong> If the
        cave's Player keeps Gravity Scale 0 and you add an impulse, the character rises and
        <em>never stops rising</em>. There is no error and no warning; the game simply has no
        ceiling. When a jump "works but feels wrong", check Gravity Scale and Body Type before
        you touch your code.
    </div>
</div>


<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain Gravity Scale, Body Type and Mass as three separate ideas, not one</li>
        <li>Say where the cave's gravity gets turned on, and prove the top-down world still works</li>
        <li>Compare the four ways to leave the ground and defend the one you wrote</li>
        <li>Explain Force versus Impulse in feel-words</li>
        <li>Explain the parabola: a velocity that gravity spends, an apex, and a fall</li>
        <li>Explain <code>GetKeyDown</code> versus <code>GetKey</code> for one-shot actions</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

